# Permutation Puzzle

> **The second environment in my RL Playground: a small puzzle that became much more complicated once I asked an agent to solve it.**

The RL Playground is my attempt to learn reinforcement learning by building environments rather than only studying algorithms in isolation.

The idea is simple:

keep creating small worlds, give agents different ways of interacting with them, measure what happens, and gradually move from hand designed behavior toward actual learning.

The first environment was a guessing game.

For the second one, I wanted something that looked simple on the surface but would force me to think differently about:

search, memory, constraints, and information.

That's where the Permutation Puzzle came from.

---

## The Idea

The puzzle is deliberately simple.

There is a hidden permutation of a set of items.

The agent submits a permutation as a guess, and receives only one piece of information:

how many elements are in the correct position.

For example, if the hidden permutation is:

[A, B, C, D]

and the agent guesses:

[A, C, D, B]

the feedback is:

1

The agent never gets told which position was correct.

That small restriction is what makes the problem interesting.

I first thought of it as a straightforward guessing problem.

Once I started building agents, it became a problem of maintaining constraints, deciding which guesses were useful, and dealing with a search space that grows factorially.

And I wanted to see how far I could take it.

---

## Why I Built It Anyway

At the beginning, I did not have a complete algorithm for solving the puzzle.

I also didn't know exactly how I wanted the final agent to work.

That wasn't a reason to wait.

One of the main reasons I started the RL Playground was to practice taking an idea that I don't completely know how to implement yet, and trying to bring it to life anyway.

So I started with simple agents.

Then I made them more intelligent.

Then I benchmarked them.

Then I made the problem bigger.

Then they broke.

That became the actual experiment.

---

## From Random Guessing to Reasoning

The first agents represented different ways of approaching the same puzzle:

* **Random Agent** — explore the space without reasoning about feedback.

* **Systematic Agent** — maintain previous guesses and reject candidates inconsistent with the observed constraints.

* **Logic Agent** — use the best known guess as a starting point and search locally through swaps.

* **Hybrid Agent** — attempt to combine constraint filtering, local search, and information based selection.

I didn't know beforehand which strategy would win.

In fact, I expected the Hybrid Agent to be the strongest because it combined the ideas of the others.

That assumption became something I could actually test.

---

## Benchmarking Before Scaling

Once the agents were working, I immediately built a benchmark instead of relying on individual games.

I wanted to know:

* How quickly does each agent solve the puzzle?

* How consistent is it?

* How does performance change as the number of elements increases?

* Does a more complicated strategy actually perform better?

The benchmark went through a few iterations itself.

Some visualizations were useful. Others weren't.

Aggregating game histories produced noisy curves that looked more meaningful than they actually were;

so I changed the evaluation to focus on solve rate by step and average steps to success.

The benchmark became part of the development process rather than something I only ran at the end.

It gave me a way to see what the agents were actually doing.

---

## Then I Increased the Search Space

With four elements, there are only:

4! = 24 possible permutations.

The agents performed well enough that I decided to push the problem to ten elements:

10! = 3,628,800 possible permutations

That change exposed weaknesses that were almost invisible at four elements.

Some early implementations tried to represent the permutation space directly, which obviously became impractical.

Other problems were more subtle.

I had made assumptions about what score changes meant for individual positions;

But the feedback only tells me a total number of correct positions.

A score changing from 3 to 2 does not tell me which position became wrong.

The feedback is a global constraint, not local evidence.

That distinction ended up becoming one of the most important lessons of the entire module.

---

## The Agent I Expected to Win Didn't

The scaling benchmark produced an outcome I wasn't expecting.

The Logic Agent performed dramatically better than the Systematic and Hybrid approaches on the larger problem.

That forced me to look beyond the names I had given the algorithms and examine what they were actually doing.

The Logic Agent was searching locally around its best known guess.

The other approaches relied much more heavily on finding valid candidates through global sampling.

As the constraints accumulated, valid candidates became increasingly rare.

So the supposedly more sophisticated Hybrid Agent was spending its effort searching a huge space for candidates that were becoming increasingly difficult to find.

That was a much more useful result than simply discovering that one agent had a better score.

It told me why my intuition about the algorithm had been wrong.

---

## I Wasn't Done Changing Things

The next stage wasn't a clean implementation of a final design.

I found more problems.

For example, the Hybrid Agent was marking candidates as "tried" when it merely considered them; rather than when it actually submitted them.

It was quietly destroying parts of its own search space.

There were also unbounded search loops that were acceptable in a small experiment but became questionable once I imagined the same logic running millions of times inside a training loop.

Even the benchmark itself had a flaw:

the survival curve could treat a timeout on the final step as if the agent had successfully solved the puzzle.

So before putting reinforcement learning on top of the environment, I stopped and audited the foundation.

That led to a principle I now want to carry into the rest of the playground:

> **A learning agent trained on a flawed environment won't reveal the flaw. It will just learn to exploit it.**

---

## From Heuristics to Reinforcement Learning

Once the environment was trustworthy enough, I started asking a different question:

**How would I represent this puzzle so that an RL agent could actually learn to solve it?**

The obvious action, submitting an entire permutation gives an action space of:

n!

That becomes unreasonable very quickly.

So instead of asking the policy to construct an entire permutation in one action, I started with a much smaller action:

**choose one swap to perform on the current permutation.**

This was directly inspired by the Logic Agent.

The difference was that the Logic Agent chooses its swaps through hand designed rules.

The RL agent would learn which swap to choose.

That felt like a much more natural first bridge from my heuristic agents into reinforcement learning.

The observation design went through several attempts too.

Some ideas were too weak.

Others were unnecessarily complicated.

One deterministic approach looked reasonable but completely collapsed when tested because it treated failure of one modified candidate as evidence against an entire position.

That was the same conceptual mistake I had already encountered earlier, appearing in a different form.

Rather than endlessly patching it, I dropped the idea.

That became a recurring pattern throughout this project:

if an approach is fundamentally wrong, changing the implementation isn't always the answer;

Sometimes the idea itself needs to go.

---

## And Then I Found Mastermind

While trying to understand how to approach the puzzle more rigorously, I eventually discovered something slightly humbling:

people had already spent a lot of time solving problems extremely similar to this one.

The puzzle belongs to the same family of problems as Mastermind, particularly permutation style variants where the feedback reports how many positions are correct.

That led me to the work around Knuth's Mastermind algorithm.

Instead of continuing to invent increasingly complicated heuristics from scratch, I now want to understand how established Mastermind solvers approach:

* candidate elimination,
* constraint satisfaction,
* minimax selection,
* information gain,
* and choosing guesses that reduce the remaining uncertainty.

I'm currently studying that approach with the intention of implementing it myself and comparing it against the agents I built.

Finding an established solution didn't make the earlier work pointless.

If anything, it gave me something much more valuable:

a reference point for understanding what my own experiments were approximating, getting wrong, or missing entirely.

---

## What This Module Became

I started this environment because I wanted another game for my RL Playground.

It turned into a much broader experiment in how to approach problems I don't fully understand yet.

I didn't start with the best algorithm.

I didn't know that Mastermind research was relevant.

I made assumptions that turned out to be wrong.

I wrote code that had to be thrown away.

I changed the benchmark after realizing it was misleading.

I changed the agent architecture after the experiments contradicted my expectations.

I tried an observation representation, tested it, watched it fail, and abandoned it.

And eventually, instead of trying to make my own heuristics increasingly complicated, I stepped back and started studying algorithms designed for this class of problem.

That's the part of the project I actually want to preserve.

---

## The Point of the Playground

The RL Playground isn't meant to demonstrate that I already know how to build sophisticated RL agents.

It's meant to document the process of getting there.

I want to be comfortable taking an idea that seems interesting, even when I don't yet have all the skills needed to execute it, and starting anyway.

Build something.

Test it.

Find out where the intuition was wrong.

Change the design.

Learn what I was missing.

Build again.

The Permutation Puzzle is probably the clearest example of that process so far.

And the next experiment is waiting:

**Can I take what I learned from the heuristic agents and the Mastermind literature, implement a principled solver, and then see what reinforcement learning can actually add on top of it?**
